                        "review": "[h1]An Unremarkable Space Shooter That Offers More Grind Than Fun[/h1]\n[b]INSPACE 2980[/b] is one of those games that isn’t outright terrible, but it’s also very hard to recommend. Unless you’re absolutely desperate to play every space-themed bullet hell on Steam, there’s not much here that stands out.\n\nThe main thing I can praise is the weapon variety. Different weapons feel distinct enough, and you can experiment with different strategies depending on the mission. That part is genuinely decent.\n\nUnfortunately, that’s where the positives end.\n\nAfter a few missions, the game started giving me a strange visual drift effect. I’m not sure whether it’s caused by the scrolling background, enemy movement, or the overall pacing, but whenever I looked away from the screen, everything around me seemed to drift to the right. I’ve played plenty of bullet hell games and never experienced anything like this before. It became genuinely annoying.\n\nI’m also not a fan of the red downgrade pickups. They feel like a pointless punishment for playing aggressively at close range, especially when using the lightning weapon. Instead of encouraging interesting risk-reward decisions, they mostly discourage a more active playstyle.\n\nAnother issue is readability. Enemy bullets and collectible resources are not outlined clearly enough, and in hectic fights they can easily blend together. For a bullet hell game, that’s a pretty significant problem.\n\nBut the biggest flaw is the progression system. I reached the second-to-last mission on the lowest difficulty and simply couldn’t complete the objective where you have to protect a ship. It didn’t seem to matter whether I destroyed every enemy I could see or deliberately tanked projectiles—the ship still got destroyed and the mission failed.\n\nThe game’s apparent solution is to replay earlier missions over and over to grind upgrade resources. That quickly turns into a chore. What makes it worse is that I’m already near the end of the campaign, yet my ship upgrades aren’t even halfway completed. The resources earned from normal mission completion are simply not enough. I finished my time with this game feeling more annoyed than entertained.\n[list]\n    [*]♉For more reviews like this, check out my[b] [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/10745643/]curator page[/url][/b].\n[/list]",
